      "text": "To find the area of the parallelogram generated by two vectors, we can use the magnitude of the cross product of the vectors. Let's denote the vectors as \\(\\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nFirst, we need to compute the cross product \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}\\). The cross product of two vectors \\(\\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} a_1 \\\\ a_2 \\\\ a_3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} b_1 \\\\ b_2 \\\\ b_3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is given by the determinant of the following matrix:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = \\begin{vmatrix} \\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ a_1 & a_2 & a_3 \\\\ b_1 & b_2 & b_3 \\end{vmatrix} = \\mathbf{i} \\left( a_2 b_3 - a_3 b_2 \\right) - \\mathbf{j} \\left( a_1 b_3 - a_3 b_1 \\right) + \\mathbf{k} \\left( a_1 b_2 - a_2 b_1 \\right).\n\\]\nSubstituting the values of \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{b}\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = \\begin{vmatrix} \\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 4 \\end{vmatrix} = \\mathbf{i} \\left( 1 \\cdot 4 - (-2) \\cdot (-3) \\right) - \\mathbf{j} \\left( 3 \\cdot 4 - (-2) \\cdot 1 \\right) + \\mathbf{k} \\left( 3 \\cdot (-3) - 1 \\cdot 1 \\right).\n\\]\nSimplifying each component, we have:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = \\mathbf{i} (4 - 6) - \\mathbf{j} (12 + 2) + \\mathbf{k} (-9 - 1) = \\mathbf{i} (-2) - \\mathbf{j} (14) + \\mathbf{k} (-10) = \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -10 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThe magnitude of the cross product vector \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}\\) is:\n\\[\n\\|\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}\\| = \\sqrt{(-2)^2 + (-14)^2 + (-10)^2} = \\sqrt{4 + 196 + 100} = \\sqrt{300} = 10\\sqrt{3}.\n\\]\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram is \\(\\boxed{10\\sqrt{3}}\\).",
