    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the area of the parallelogram generated by the vectors \\(\\mathbf{u} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need to calculate the magnitude of their cross product \\(\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}\\).\n\nThe cross product \\(\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}\\) is given by the determinant of the following matrix:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\begin{vmatrix}\n\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\\n3 & 1 & -2 \\\\\n1 & -3 & 4\n\\end{vmatrix}\n\\]\n\nWe can expand this determinant along the first row:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{i} \\begin{vmatrix} 1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 4 \\end{vmatrix} - \\mathbf{j} \\begin{vmatrix} 3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\end{vmatrix} + \\mathbf{k} \\begin{vmatrix} 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\end{vmatrix}\n\\]\n\nNow, we calculate each of the 2x2 determinants:\n\\[\n\\begin{vmatrix} 1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 4 \\end{vmatrix} = (1)(4) - (-2)(-3) = 4 - 6 = -2\n\\]\n\\[\n\\begin{vmatrix} 3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\end{vmatrix} = (3)(4) - (-2)(1) = 12 + 2 = 14\n\\]\n\\[\n\\begin{vmatrix} 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\end{vmatrix} = (3)(-3) - (1)(1) = -9 - 1 = -10\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these values back into the expression for the cross product, we get:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{i}(-2) - \\mathbf{j}(14) + \\mathbf{k}(-10) = \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -10 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThe magnitude of the vector \\(\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}\\) is:\n\\[\n\\|\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}\\| = \\sqrt{(-2)^2 + (-14)^2 + (-10)^2} = \\sqrt{4 + 196 + 100} = \\sqrt{300} = 10\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram is \\(\\boxed{10\\sqrt{3}}\\).",
